{"kind":"request","text":"Please review the two-process fix at this head, by 4d873371. The planner put it first. A card whose calls reach another process of the same home now sends its own opening call there and reads again. It never repeats a press.\n\n**What to check first.**\n- `packages/mcp/browser/main.ts`: `reopen`, `isAbsent`, and the two places that use them. The press path must return with no second `view_act`.\n- `packages/mcp/src/views/session.ts`: the refusal's new words.\n- `packages/mcp/src/views/declared.ts`: `cardFor`. A declared card carried the first built-in card's bytes, so it could not name its own opening tool. The digest now covers its own bytes, and the generation of a declared package changes.\n\n**The planner's questions, answered in the note.** The note weighs a reopen, view state on disk, and one owner process for held reads, pending acts and an act that must not go twice. It also says how custody behaves under two processes. Please press on item 3 of that section: two processes can both pass the custody check in the moment before either keeps its record. Then the scope's revision check is the last guard, and these tests use a stand-in that judges nothing.\n\n**What the runs show, and what they do not.**\n- The first form of the test failed at main by the app's exact words, with two real processes on one home.\n- Three tests hold the new behaviour on real processes, including a lost reply and a press while the other process waits.\n- The card ran in Chromium with two services in one host process, 28 of 28 cases.\n- Two real processes ran on `dap-demo` in the J2 room, under `agent-a`, with a script in the app's place. One press signed one permanent `read-token` act.\n- No desktop AI app has rendered this. No log from the app names which process took which call, so the routing is still a reading.\n\n**The workflow citation, by 0998f9ec.** Run 41332615607 at `1dc134fd`. `remote/run.txt` holds its steps.\n\n**Limits you may want to weigh.** A